   __SS__ee__cc__tt__ii__oo__nn __22__..____II__nn__ss__tt__aa__ll__ll__aa__tt__ii__oo__nn____________ 

   From the overview document, you know that OCT reads a class
   file, imports public interface files for classes you reference,
   and then outputs a "C" file ready for your "C" compiler.  Where
   these things come from and where the go are determined by
   environment variables.  For the Amiga system, Manx's _s_e_t is
   used, not the ENV: directory (sorry).  For UNIX users, use the
   normal shell environment variables.  

        To get the system up and ready for class development
   requires a sub-directory with a few directories.  You may elect
   to not mix OCT header and library files with the rest of your
   system.  Here is a basic roadmap for the directories: 


          ---> OOC -----+----- methods
                        |
                        +----- include ---+--- objc
                        |                 |
                        |                 +--- apps
                        |                 |
                        |                 +--- exec (for UNIX only)
                        |
                        |
        (from here on, these directories may mingle..)
                        |
                        +----- lib
                        |
                        +----- bin



        FFiigg.. 22--11,, OCT Directory Tree     .  


   Into the 'methods' directory you should copy all the *.m files
   from this directory on the distribution disk.  These serve as
   good examples of how to call various services.  

        The 'include' directory contains the public interface
   header files for classes in your OCT library.  Copy this
   directory and its subdirectories verbatim from the distribution
   disk.  For UNIX people, the eexxeecc directory contains files
   defining the list and node primitive structures used internally
   to the OCT translator and the application substrate.  Assign the
   variable INCLUDE to also specify this directory.  


   Barney - OCT                  pg. 3                   29-Jul-1993


   Object C Translator                                     Release 2



        The remaining directories can be seperate from your system
   directories if you don't want "mixing" of programs.  A temporary
   directory may be specified with the variable CCTEMP.  If not
   specified, a default directory is used, depending on the host
   system (AmigaDOS, UNIX or MS-DOS).  
















































