   In the object oriented world, the programmer dreams of objects
   that actions affect.  Objects are usually modeled after real
   life entities.  "Look for structure, other systems, devices,
   things remembered, roles played, locations, organization units,
   and descriptive catalogs." [Coad89] "As a system evolves, its
   tasks may change drastically.  Much more persistency may be
   found in the classes of data it manipulates.." [Meyer88] These
   objects may maintain state internally, or relie on other objects
   to remember.  

        Objects are instances of a class, and all objects of a
   given class react similarily (although, based on state,
   reactions may be different).  Each class will be a problem
   solving module.  "Modular concepts apply to programming as well
   as specification and design." [Meyer88].  


   44..11.. SSaammppllee SSyysstteemm:: QQuueeuuee     

        This paper will outline the development of the QQuueeuuee
   class.  This queue is single ended, circular, and will expand to
   meet its own size requirements.  This class basically joins
   OOrrddeerreeddCCoolllleeccttiioonn''ss input and adding abilities to SSeeqquueennccee''ss
   output and enumeration methods.  It will inherit from the
   abstract class CCoolllleeccttiioonn directly; this should keep minimal any
   awkwardness trying to shoehorn the QQuueeuuee class on top of another
   class.  You should have a copy of the CCoolllleeccttiioonn class method
   file (.m) on handy during your reading here.  

















   Barney - OCT                  pg. 6                   29-Jul-1993


   Object C Translator                                     Release 2





   SSeeccttiioonn 55 
   SSeeccttiioonn 55 
   __SS__ee__cc__tt__ii__oo__nn __55__..____CC__ll__aa__ss__ss____FF__ii__ll__ee__________ 

   **** THIS DOCUMENT IS UNFINISHED **** 

   To create a class requires the creation of two files: the
   interface and the implementation.  The interface file is a
   public header that all clients of the class will read.  The
   implementation is where the code to support the class is
   located.  This file will be described first.  


   55..11.. MMeetthhooddss       

        Time to invoke your favorite program editor and start
   writing.  Normally you would invoke your favorite pencil and
   start writing on paper.  Planning ahead on which methods a class
   will implemenet is very important, as they will dictate what
   type of services the class will be providing, and hence, what
   data structures will be used.  For the QQuueeuuee we'll be adding to
   the front and removing from the tail.  Any absolute references
   will have to be adjusted to fit around the circular-ness of the
   Queue.  Only some of the conversion methods (ie, asBag) will be
   implemented here, but you may flesh out the class completely.  

        Examining the CCoolllleeccttiioonn method file, you'll notice the add
   and remove methods are very generic.  These will be interpreted
   as "addHead:" and "remTail:" 

   The _p_r_e_l_i_n_k_e_r builds a list of modules provided by the user.
   When the runtime system initializes itself, it walks down this
   list finding all the dependent classes.  From this list, all the
   methods are given codes, and every class initialized.  


   55..22.. AAfftteerr TTrraannssllaattiioonn      

        A prelinker is used to build a list of classes that will be
   brought together to form the final application.  A small ..cc file
   is built, which must be compiled and added into the list of
   object modules for the appliction.  This list tells the linker
   which classes to extract from the library, including any
   dependencies the included classes need, like other classes.
   It's also used by the substrate at startup-time to build a
   central list of method selector codes and class names.
   Normally, this small file need only be rebuilt when the list of
   modules the user provides changes in some way, or a new class is
   added to the application.  


